as an immigrant from chile pierre bravo  spent many years undocumented and in the  shadows she worked odd jobs to pay her  way through school before finding an  opening in the media industry and  eventually becoming a reporter on msnbc  in her new book the other how to own  your power at work as a woman of color  she writes about her journey and the  lessons she's learned along the way  daniela pierre-bravo good morning to you  thank you for being here good morning  thank you so much for having me you know  what i love about the book is that you  talk about as an immigrant that there  are really great things about entering  the workplace of hustling and having  this work ethic but then you talk about  how that can  change and it's not so great anymore  yeah well the immigrant ethos is a  beautiful thing right my mom and my  grandma always taught me to put my head  down and do the work right be grateful  for everything it's the energy that got  me from undocumented to be able to go to  college to getting on an 18 hour bus  ride because i lied on my resume and  said that i lived in new york just that  i could get an opportunity to get an  unpaid internship  but you know by the same token when we  advance in our career especially for  women of color when we're constantly  leading with that overly grateful  mentality to the point where we fear  asking for more because we don't want to  be ungrateful it can kind of not work in  our favor and by the way if you put your  head down and do the work in corporate  america if you don't let people know  what you're doing yeah no one's going to  notice that you got to brag about  yourself a little bit don't be humble uh  the book uh speaking of not being humble  i'll not be humble for you you got a  great voice uh you got a delicate touch  it's gonna help a lot of people uh and  you describe a moment in the book it's  sort of part career advice uh and then  part memoir and in the memoir part you  talk about a breaking point for yourself  when you started thinking about change  can you talk about that breaking point  yeah i think for a lot of women of color  we experience this breaking point of  burnout  and you know for a lot of our  communities we have been in constant  survival mode that fight or flight is  very familiar to us so we tend to find  and recreate those moments in our career  to the point where we feel like our  worth  is predicated on our productivity and  it's hard for us to shift that mindset  and we know that as women go on in their  careers we need to play by different  rules we can't be constantly in that  customer service mentality  and it's hard because that means that we  need to say no to some things that we  advise we do that and it's hard right  and especially for women of color  because we don't want to cause that  cognitive dissonance we don't want to  put people at this ease but it's so  important as we move along in our career  you know i really try to cheerlead other  women yeah but i even find it hard to  cheerlead myself what are some tips for  people that you would if you're speaking  to other women that you give them yeah i  mean i think that you know  we're so lucky now especially gen z as  we as we you know as they come up in the  workplace gen z is going to be the most  diverse generation out there and when  i think about as  a woman of color who is sometimes the  one and only in a room it's not just  about me  right it's about the advocating that i  do for myself but the the the more room  that i leave for other women behind us  and i think that's important that we're  not just cheerleading ourselves and it's  not just about us but it's about the  door that we leave even wider for the  women after us yeah pay matters pay  negotiations yeah yeah uh what tips do  you have on that front and are you right  about why even if you get a no at first  you shouldn't stop it no yeah there's a  lot of great tips on negotiation in the  book but that's exactly right it's so  important to not leave that negotiation  and just assume the no is forever so  what you want to do is you want to make  sure to talk to your manager and say  okay what are the metrics that i missed  and what are the qualifications and the  metrics that you're looking for so that  they meet them and make sure that you  don't leave there without putting a time  on their calendar whether it's three or  six months to have this conversation to  check in because you don't want to wait  a full year for the next performance  review yeah you really have to know your  worth though sometimes that can be  really hard yeah well that's why it's so  important to talk about it and talk  about it often you know in this remote  work kind of life that we're going  through it's even more important it's  it's more than okay it's necessary and  you know it's hard to be a manager at  this time there's a lot of things that  they're juggling so  help them help you and one way by doing  that is to make sure that you're having  consistent conversations whether it be  weekly or bi-weekly about what are the  wins that you're bringing to the table i  like that a lot daniella prier bravo  thank you so much for being here we  really appreciate it so much the other  how to own your power at work as a woman  of color goes on sale tomorrow you can  pick it up
 

